course: geography
lesson: 7
title: Europe: countries and capitals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Start EU5 in 1337 and Europe is a patchwork: the Holy Roman Empire alone holds hundreds of states, and Italy is Venice, Genoa, Milan, Florence, Naples and the Pope's lands.
explain: Today's map is much simpler, about 44 to 50 countries, and even that number depends on whether you count Russia, Turkey, Kazakhstan and Kosovo.
explain: Many countries you know are younger than you think: Italy became one state in 1861 and Germany in 1871, long after the game's Holy Roman Empire.
explain: Capitals move for reasons: Philip II put Spain's court in central Madrid, and Warsaw took over from Kraków when Poland was joined with Lithuania.
explain: Some countries are odd: the Netherlands' capital is Amsterdam but its government sits in The Hague, and Switzerland has no capital by law at all.
fact: Europe has about 44 to 50 countries, depending on whether partly European countries such as Russia, Turkey and Kazakhstan, and disputed places such as Kosovo, are counted.
fact: Russia, with its capital Moscow, is the largest country in Europe and the world, and although only about a quarter of its land is in Europe, most of its people live there.
fact: Vatican City, inside Rome, is the smallest country in the world at about 0.44 square kilometres, and it is ruled by the Pope; it is all that is left of the Pope's lands, the rest of which joined Italy by 1870.
fact: Germany, with its capital Berlin, has the most people of any country wholly in Europe, about 84 million.
fact: Spain's capital is Madrid, which Philip II made the permanent seat of the court in 1561 partly because it lies near the centre of the country.
fact: Italy's capital is Rome, though the country became one state only in 1861, with Rome joining in 1870.
fact: Poland's capital is Warsaw, which took over from Kraków around 1596, partly because it lay nearer the middle of Poland and Lithuania joined together.
fact: The capital of the Netherlands is Amsterdam, but the government, parliament and the king's working palace are in The Hague.
fact: Switzerland has no official capital by law, and Bern is called the federal city, where the government and parliament meet; the country is made of 26 cantons (regions), and having no capital keeps them all equal.
fact: Ukraine, with its capital Kyiv, is the largest country wholly in Europe, at about 600,000 square kilometres.
fact: The European Union has 27 member countries after the United Kingdom left in 2020, and 21 of them use the euro since Bulgaria joined it in 2026.
fact: Portugal's capital is Lisbon, and Portugal's land border with Spain has stayed almost unchanged since a treaty in 1297, among the oldest in Europe; its only land neighbour was the bigger kingdom of Castile, so Portugal grew by sea.
fact: Germany became one state only in 1871; in the EU5 period its land was split among the many states of the Holy Roman Empire.
fact: In 1337 Spain did not exist yet: Castile, Aragon, Navarre and Muslim Granada were separate, and Castile and Aragon were joined by the marriage of Isabella and Ferdinand in 1469.
exercise: About how many countries does Europe have? | About 44 to 50, depending on what is counted | Exactly 27 | About 12 | More than 100
exercise: Which is the largest country in Europe and the world? | Russia | Ukraine | France | Canada
exercise: Where do most Russians live? | In the European part of Russia | In Siberia, near the big forests | Along the Pacific coast | In the Arctic
exercise: Which is the smallest country in the world? | Vatican City | Liechtenstein | San Marino | Malta
exercise: Who rules Vatican City? | The Pope | The King of Italy | The Mayor of Rome | The European Union
exercise: Which country wholly in Europe has the most people? | Germany | France | The United Kingdom | Italy
exercise: Who made Madrid the permanent seat of Spain's court in 1561? | Philip II | Ferdinand and Isabella | Napoleon Bonaparte | Charles Martel
exercise: When did Italy become one state? | In 1861 | In 1337 | In 1918 | In 476
exercise: Which city was Poland's capital before Warsaw? | Kraków | Gdańsk | Vilnius | Poznań
exercise: Where do the Dutch government and parliament meet? | The Hague | Amsterdam | Rotterdam | Brussels
exercise: What is Bern officially called? | The federal city | The capital by law | The royal city | The free city
exercise: Which is the largest country lying entirely within Europe? | Ukraine | France | Germany | Spain
exercise: What is Ukraine's capital? | Kyiv | Lviv in the west | Odesa | Kharkiv
exercise: How many countries are in the European Union now? | 27 | 28 | 21 | 50
exercise: How many EU countries use the euro since Bulgaria joined in 2026? | 21 | 27 | 19 | 12
exercise: What is special about the border between Portugal and Spain? | It has barely changed since 1297 | It was drawn up by the winners in 1945 | It moves every century | It is the newest border in Europe
exercise: When did Germany become one state? | In 1871 | In 1337 | In 1648 | In 1990 for the first time
test: Why can two atlases give different numbers of European countries? | They count partly European and disputed places differently | One atlas is always out of date | Countries change every year | Some atlases forget the islands
test: In EU5 you play Castile in 1337. Why is there no "Spain" on the map? | Spain was formed later by joining Castile, Aragon and others | Spain was part of France then | The game left Spain out by mistake | Spain was under the sea
test: Name three EU5 states that are now part of Italy. | Venice, Genoa and Naples | Venice, Bavaria and Bohemia | Milan, Provence and Navarre | Florence, Austria and Bohemia
test: What happened to the rest of the Papal States? | They joined Italy, leaving the Pope only the Vatican | They became Switzerland | They still form a big country | They became part of France
test: Why might a king choose a capital in the middle of his lands? | It is easier to reach and rule every part from there | The middle of a country is always on the coast | It is further from the sun | Kings were not allowed to live by the sea in case of pirates
test: Why did the union with Lithuania make Warsaw a better capital than Kraków? | Warsaw lay nearer the middle of the joined lands | Kraków was handed over to Lithuania | Warsaw had the only river in Poland | Lithuania owned Kraków
test: amsterdam's the capital so the king and parliament are there yeah? | No, they work in The Hague | Yes, all of them sit in Amsterdam | No, they work in Brussels | Yes, but only in summer
test: Why might the Swiss avoid naming an official capital? | To keep the cantons equal, with no city above the others | Because Switzerland has no cities | Because Bern is in France | Because the Pope forbids it in the old treaty with the Swiss
test: Is every EU country part of the euro area? | No, 21 of the 27 use the euro | Yes, all 27 use it | No, only 6 use it | Yes, and so does the UK
test: Why did Portugal look to the ocean rather than to land to grow? | Its only land neighbour was the stronger Castile | It had no ports | The ocean was smaller then | Portugal had no army at all
test: In EU5 Muscovy keeps growing east. Why is Russia still called European though most of its land is in Asia? | Its capital and most of its people are in Europe | Asia belongs to Europe | Siberia has always been counted as part of Europe | Russia has no land in Asia
exam: Identify the sovereign state of least territorial extent in the world. | Vatican City | Monaco | Liechtenstein | Luxembourg
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the seat of government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands? | The government sits at The Hague, though Amsterdam is the capital | The government sits at Amsterdam, which is the capital | The government sits at Brussels | The government sits at Rotterdam
exam: Ascertain the year in which Italy was constituted as a single state. | Eighteen sixty-one | Thirteen thirty-seven | Nineteen eighteen | Eighteen fifteen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the largest state situated wholly within Europe. | It is Ukraine | It is France | It is Germany | It is Russia
